Some keys of user interface (e.g. keywords, student-id)
contains special chars, resulting the storage macro
is not convenient to evoke (by `\@nameuse`),
so here we add a (inner) macro `\swufe@define@key`
to set some meta info of the key. Currently we support
the alias field,
```latex
\swufe@define@key{
  keywords* = {
    alias = keywords@en
  },
  student-id = {
    alias = student@id
  },
}
```

Breaking Changes Here.

Improved the Websocket architecture so that it is easy to override and easy to piggy back on for your own custom websocket needs. It also now reuses the same http ports that the main app uses, rather than having to open new ports to support websockets.

Added a database configuration file, which from here on out will be the official way to pass in database credentials into the app for deployment. This way, you do not need to add your server's database credentials to the source code. It also gives you the ability to do local docker builds and control how the database is accessed. This is especially important for Mac users, since Docker Desktop is implemented in a way that requires a special server name to get to the host's database server.

Fixed a security risk that potentially held database credentials in ram.

Also improved the docker deployment process, and simplified some of the startup process.

This is going to require the following changes to already existing projects:

-  Remove globals that are no longer used. Doing a build will immediately show which ones, but they are: config.UseFCGI, config.WebSocketPort, config.WebSocketTLSPort, config.WebSocketTLSKeyFile, config.WebSocketTLSCertFile
-  Add the following to the Application.MakeServerMux function:
```
	// Serve up the websocket messenger
	mux.Handle(config.WebsocketMessengerPrefix, http.HandlerFunc(app.WebsocketMessengerHandler))

```

To take advantage of the new dbConfigFile, you will need to do the following:

- add the following lines to the top of the main.go file in the global flags section:
```
// dbConfigFile is now read in config, but we define it here so it can be part of the usage message, though it is not used here
var dbConfigFile = flag.String("dbConfigFile", "", "The path to the database configuration file.")
```

Then change how you setup your database(s) in config/db.go to something like this:
```
func addMyDatabase() {
	var configOverrides map[string]interface{}

	// Use our own flag getter since the flags have not been read yet
	dbConfigFile, _ := sys.GetFlagString("-dbConfigFile")

	if dbConfigFile != "" {
		var err error

		configOverrides, err = stringmap.FromJsonFile(dbConfigFile)
		if err != nil {
			panic ("Database configuration file error: " + err.Error())
		}
	}

	cfg := mysql.NewConfig()

	// Local development credentials
	key := "mydb"
	cfg.DBName = "mydbname"
	cfg.User = "root"
	cfg.Passwd = "12345"
	cfg.ParseTime = true

	// These are overridden by the database config file if one is specified on the command line.
	// The recommended way to secure database
	// credentials in your release version of the app is to store them in this json file
	// that only the launcher of the app can read. On unix, you would `chmod 400 pw_file` to
	// set the permission of the file. The app then will read the file above and use it
	// to set the credentials. If using docker, mount the file into the docker container.
	//
	// The format of the file should be:
	// {
	// 	  "put_db_key_here": {
	//		"dbname": "database name here",
	//		"user": "database user name here",
	//		etc.
	// 	 See MysqlOverrideConfigSettings for more info.
	//
	if i,ok := configOverrides[key]; ok {
		db.MysqlOverrideConfigSettings(cfg, i.(map[string]interface{}))
	}

	db1 := db.NewMysql5(key, "", cfg)

	if !config.Release {
		db1.StartProfiling()
	}

	db.AddDatabase(db1, key)
}

```

Addresses https://github.com/GSA/sam-design-system/issues/467.

USWDS hides browser input by applying ` {
    position: absolute;
    left: -999em;
}` to push the input far to the left. 

sds-dialog-container has a position defined, this position is inherited by its non-positioned descendants on all browsers except Firefox. Per [MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/position#absolute), "It [an element with position:absolute] is positioned relative to its closest positioned ancestor, if any; otherwise, it is placed relative to the initial containing block." As a result of that behavior and the lack of the position being inherited by descendants on Firefox, the inputs try and position themselves relative to their closest positioned ancestor, resulting in the double scrollbar

